data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR gene editing modifies the genomes of living organisms and is based on a simplified version of the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 antiviral defense system [1].\n- Cas9 nuclease, complexed with a synthetic guide RNA (gRNA), is delivered into a cell, which cuts the genome at a desired location [1].\n- Genome editing inserts, deletes, modifies, or replaces DNA at site-specific locations, using programmable molecules that create double-strand breaks [3].\n- In prokaryotes, CRISPR sequences derive from bacteriophage DNA and help detect and destroy similar phage DNA during later infections [2].\n- Headlines report a CRISPR breakthrough aimed at treating a broad array of diseases [4], and HIV eliminated in cell culture using CRISPR-Cas editing [5]."}

